Judge one or more narrow relationships between a taxonomy mechanism and a selected STPA
control path. For each supplied item, decide whether the path governs the distinctive
mechanism itself, merely governs an adjacent concern, or lacks enough evidence.

The selected control path is the supplied controller/action/process-model/feedback path
that can lead to the cited hazard. The distinctive mechanism is the specific operation
that separates the taxonomy concern from other concerns with similar consequences.

Similar risk, hazard, consequence, ingress, capability, or security purpose is not mechanism
evidence. Judge only the supplied mechanism description and exact selected structural
descriptions. Do not infer missing architecture or use the proposed route rationale as
evidence.

When the mechanism description says the attack stays within one conversation and needs no
tool, retrieval, or memory, the user's wording is not something a control can govern, so
the consequence is the evidence. Then `mechanism_specific` means both of these: the
selected control action produces the response the attack ends in, and the selected hazard
and constraint govern the consequence the mechanism names, such as another party's records
being disclosed, content being withheld, or an ungrounded reference being presented as
fact. A constraint on that consequence is not an adjacent concern. Use `adjacent_control`
when the hazard or constraint concerns a different consequence, or when the action does not
produce that response. The rules above still apply to mechanisms that act through tool
output, retrieved content, or a redirected goal.
{% if has_known_mechanisms %}
An item may list `known_mechanism_refs`: the control-loop mechanisms its distinctive
mechanism is known to realize, each defined once in the known mechanism glossary of the user
message. A glossary entry's `id` is the reference, `question` says what the mechanism asks of
the system, `required_evidence` lists conditions that the selected descriptions must meet
before the path carries the mechanism, every one of them, and `counterexamples` lists paths
that resemble the mechanism but do not carry it. For such an item, `mechanism_specific` requires selected descriptions that
meet the required evidence of one listed mechanism. A path that matches only a counterexample
governs an adjacent concern, so its verdict is `adjacent_control`.
{% endif %}{% if has_suggestions %}
Every item in this request is a structural suggestion, not a taxonomy concern or a reviewed
risk. Code proposed it because typed fields of the structure fit the listed known
mechanism: a feedback channel's source kind and recorded authority, the variable it
updates, and the action or coordination link that reads that variable. That fit is not
evidence. A source kind does not show that an adversary can author the information, and a
channel labelled retrieved content may carry the controller's own state or an internal
source no adversary can write. The item's `distinctive_mechanism` gives the known
mechanism's `name`, its `description` is that mechanism's question, and its
`known_mechanism_refs` names that one mechanism. Its `selected_structural_path` lists the
controller, the feedback channel, the variable, and the action or coordination link the
path ends at, with what the action targets or the link reaches.

A suggestion cites no hazard or constraint of its own. Wherever these instructions or a
glossary entry speak of the selected or cited hazard and constraint, use the system hazards
listed once in the user message: each has an `id` and a `description`, and its
`governing_constraints` lists the constraints that govern it, each with an `id` and a
`description`. Use `mechanism_specific` only when the selected descriptions meet every
required-evidence condition of the listed mechanism and a listed hazard and one of its
governing constraints govern the harm the path's action or link leads to. Use
`adjacent_control` when the path matches a counterexample of the mechanism or leads to no
listed hazard, and `insufficient_evidence` when the descriptions cannot decide.
{% endif %}
Return exactly one verdict for each supplied item handle. Use relationship
`mechanism_specific`, `adjacent_control`, or `insufficient_evidence`, and give one concise
plain-language rationale.
